# onioncrawler

A **resumable, trap-resistant crawler + search engine for Tor hidden services
(`.onion` only)**. Self-contained, **Python 3.11 standard library only** — no
pip, no third-party packages. Privacy-first, no-JavaScript search UI bound to
`127.0.0.1`.

It is built to survive two things that break naive crawlers:

1. **Crashes / stops** — every byte of crawl state lives in SQLite (WAL), and
   in-flight URLs are *leased*, so `Ctrl-C`/`kill` and restart continues
   **exactly** where it left off with no lost URLs and no duplicate work.
2. **Bot traps / tarpits** — calendar bombs, infinite pagination, recursive
   path loops, duplicate-content farms, oversized responses, and hostile
   `robots`/delays are all defended against and, where structural, unit-tested.

> **Sandbox note:** there is no Tor daemon and no network in the test
> environment. Everything is testable offline against a local fixture
> "hidden service" via a `DirectFetcher` (localhost transport). The real
> transport, `TorSocksFetcher`, speaks hand-rolled SOCKS5 to a local Tor daemon.

---

## Quick start

```bash
cd /tmp/astrx-suite/onioncrawler

# 1) Run the test suite (offline, no Tor needed)
python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t . -v

# 2) Real crawl over Tor (requires a running Tor daemon on 127.0.0.1:9050)
python3 -m onioncrawler crawl --seeds seeds.txt --db crawl.db -v

# 3) Serve the no-JS search UI on localhost
python3 -m onioncrawler search --db crawl.db --port 8802
#    -> browse http://127.0.0.1:8802/  and  http://127.0.0.1:8802/api/search?q=...

# 4) Inspect state
python3 -m onioncrawler stats --db crawl.db

# 5) Submit / bulk-import seeds at runtime (validated + blocklist-checked)
python3 -m onioncrawler submit --db crawl.db abcd…xyz.onion
python3 -m onioncrawler submit --db crawl.db --file more_seeds.txt

# 6) Offline maintenance: link-graph authority, mirror clustering, recrawl
python3 -m onioncrawler authority --db crawl.db   # PageRank-lite over onion hosts
python3 -m onioncrawler cluster   --db crawl.db   # SimHash near-dup mirror clusters
python3 -m onioncrawler recrawl   --db crawl.db   # mark due pages for refetch
```

Stop a crawl any time with `Ctrl-C`; run the same `crawl` command again to
resume.

The search server also exposes `/metrics` (Prometheus text), `/health` (JSON),
a token-bucket rate limit on the public endpoints, and Basic-auth-gated admin
actions `POST /add` (submit a seed), `/purge` (block+delete a host), `/recrawl`
(enable with `--admin-user`/`--admin-pass`). Packaging lives in `pyproject.toml`,
`Dockerfile`, and `deploy/` (systemd units + an example `torrc` that publishes
the search UI as a v3 hidden service).

---

## Architecture

```
onioncrawler/
  onion.py        v3/v2 .onion + .i2p host validators + require_* anti-leak gates
  canonical.py    URL canonicalization, dedup key, template + skeleton keys
  traps.py        pure structural trap predicates (path-repeat, cycles, bombs)
  socks.py        hand-rolled SOCKS5 (RFC1928) CONNECT + user/pass (RFC1929)
  i2p.py          I2P HTTP-proxy encoders (CONNECT / absolute-form GET)
  http_client.py  minimal HTTP/1.1 over a socket: chunked, CL, gzip/deflate
  fetcher.py      Fetcher interface; TorSocksFetcher + I2PHttpFetcher + DirectFetcher
  robots.py       robots.txt parser (allow/disallow/crawl-delay, *, $)
  extract.py      html.parser: title, visible text, links, meta-robots
  abuse.py        operator abuse blocklist (hosts + keywords + media hashes) — REQUIRED
  seedlist.py     curated known-onions seed list import + scheduled re-seed
  storage.py      SQLite WAL: frontier/leasing, pages, FTS5, hosts, counters, backup
  crawler.py      the crawl engine wiring every defense together
  search.py       stdlib http.server no-JS search UI + JSON API + /opensearch.xml + /blocklist
  stats.py        human-readable stats
  config.py       every limit/knob, all CLI-overridable
  __main__.py     CLI: crawl | search | stats | submit | reseed | backup | …
tests/            fixture hidden service + unittest suite
seeds.example     example seed file (.onion / .i2p only)
blocklist_hosts.txt / blocklist_keywords.txt / blocklist_media.txt   abuse lists (ship EMPTY)
```

Data flow for one URL: `lease` → onion/abuse gate → `robots` → `fetch`
(SOCKS5→HTTP) → content-type/size gate → `extract` → abuse content gate →
dedup + `store` (FTS5) → canonicalize & enqueue links (through every trap
guard) → `mark done` → trap-score the host → set politeness clock.

---

## The Tor / onion fetcher

* **SOCKS5, hand-rolled** in `socks.py` over raw sockets. Supports *no-auth* and
  *username/password* auth (RFC 1929). The byte encoding is factored into pure
  functions (`build_greeting`, `build_userpass_auth`, `build_connect_request`)
  and unit-tested against the RFC layout.
* **Remote DNS only.** Targets are sent as SOCKS5 `ATYP=0x03` (DOMAINNAME); the
  Tor proxy resolves them. `.onion` names are **never** resolved locally — this
  is both required for onion routing and a hard anti-leak property.
* **Stream isolation.** With `stream_isolation` on (default), each host is given
  a distinct SOCKS username, so Tor (`IsolateSOCKSAuth`) builds a separate
  circuit per host.
* **Minimal HTTP/1.1** (`http_client.py`): `GET`, request headers, `chunked`
  and `Content-Length` bodies, connection-close bodies, `gzip`/`deflate`
  decompression via stdlib, and a hard `max_response_bytes` cap. Redirects are
  followed one hop at a time with a cap (`max_redirects`).
* **Onion-only enforcement, two layers.** `canonicalize()` drops any non-onion
  URL, and `require_onion()` is called again immediately before a socket is
  opened. A clearnet/invalid host is **refused and never connected to** — tested
  in `test_fetcher.py` (both `TorSocksFetcher` and `DirectFetcher`).
* **Pluggable fetchers**, selected by `--fetcher`:
  * `TorSocksFetcher` — real deployment (SOCKS5 → Tor → `.onion`).
  * `DirectFetcher` — **TESTING ONLY**, plain HTTP to a localhost fixture, with
    **no anonymity**. It still runs the onion validator and maps synthetic
    `.onion` hosts to `127.0.0.1:<port>`, so the whole pipeline is exercised
    while only the transport is swapped.

---

## Resumability (crash-safe)

All state is in **one SQLite database in WAL mode**. Nothing important lives in
process memory.

* **`frontier`** table: `url` (unique), `host`, `depth`, `status`
  (`queued|leased|done|error`), `priority`, `template`, `skeleton`,
  `enqueued_at`, `lease_expires`, `tries`.
* **Atomic lease.** A worker leases the best eligible row with a single
  `UPDATE ... WHERE id=(SELECT ... status='queued' ...) RETURNING ...` guarded
  by `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, flipping it to `leased` with a `lease_expires` in the
  future and **parking the host** for the lease window (this simultaneously
  gives per-host serialization + politeness).
* **Crash recovery.** On startup (and before every lease) expired leases are
  reclaimed: `status='leased' AND lease_expires < now → 'queued'`. An in-flight
  URL whose worker died is therefore never lost — it becomes queued again once
  its lease expires. On a *graceful* stop, all leases are returned to the queue
  immediately.
* **No refetching.** Completed URLs are `done` and are never leased again unless
  a **recrawl TTL** has elapsed (`requeue_stale`). Enqueue is `INSERT OR IGNORE`
  on a unique `url`, so a URL seen twice is deduped structurally.
* **Budgets/counters persist** (in `hosts` and `meta`), so per-host and global
  limits carry across restarts.

Tested in `test_resume.py`: a run capped to K fetched pages leaves the frontier
in a mixed `done`+`queued` state; a fresh restart resumes, **does not refetch
any `done` URL**, drains the frontier, and reaches **exactly the same final page
set** as a single uninterrupted crawl. A separate test simulates a mid-fetch
crash and asserts the expired lease is reclaimed and re-leasable.

---

## Bot-trap / tarpit defenses

Every defense is configurable (`config.py`) and the structural ones are
unit-tested (`test_canonical.py`, `test_crawl.py`).

1. **Per-host politeness + global concurrency.** Requests to a host are
   serialized via the lease-parks-host mechanism, with `crawl_delay` + random
   `crawl_delay_jitter`; `workers` caps global concurrency.
2. **robots.txt** fetched, parsed, cached per host (persisted), and honored
   (`allow`/`disallow` with `*`/`$`, `crawl-delay`). Also honors
   `<meta name=robots>` `noindex`/`nofollow` and the `X-Robots-Tag` header.
   Robots `crawl-delay` is capped (`max_robots_crawl_delay`) so a host can't
   tarpit you with a huge delay.
3. **Hard limits.** `max_depth`, `max_pages_per_host` (budget), `max_total_pages`,
   `max_response_bytes`, and a content-type allowlist (`text/html`, `text/plain`).
4. **URL canonicalization for dedup.** Lowercase host, strip default port, drop
   fragment, resolve `.`/`..`, drop known tracking params (utm_*, fbclid, gclid,
   sessionid, …), sort remaining query params → one stable key.
5. **Query-explosion guard.** A **template key** = host + path + sorted query
   *keys* (values stripped). All `/cal?year=Y&month=M` collapse to one template,
   capped at `max_urls_per_template`. Calendar/pagination bombs (every query
   value numeric/date-ish) get a tighter `pagination_numeric_cap`.
6. **Path-repetition / infinite-depth guard.** Reject paths with too many
   segments (`max_path_segments`), a segment repeated too often
   (`max_segment_repeats`), or a repeating segment *cycle* (`/a/b/a/b/a/b`).
7. **Content dedup.** SHA-1 of normalized `title+visible-text`; duplicates are
   not stored, and the per-host duplicate ratio is tracked.
8. **Trap scoring / auto-blacklist (for the run).** A host is marked `trapped`
   and its remaining queued URLs dead-lettered when it exceeds its page budget,
   or its duplicate ratio (`dup_ratio_threshold`) or error ratio
   (`error_ratio_threshold`) crosses a threshold with enough samples. The reason
   is logged in `trap_log`.
9. **Global backstops.** A total unique-URL budget (`max_unique_urls`) and a
   **skeleton** set — host + path with numeric/hex/date segments collapsed to
   `#` — capped by `max_urls_per_skeleton` catches id-parameterized page farms.

Every drop/decision is written to the `trap_log` table (see `stats`).

---

## Abuse filtering — REQUIRED operator duty

Like the [Ahmia](https://ahmia.fi/) project, a legitimate onion search index
**must** filter abusive material. This is a first-class, tested code path, not
an afterthought.

* **`blocklist_hosts.txt`** — one `.onion` host per line. Any page on a listed
  host is dropped and the host is blacklisted for the run.
* **`blocklist_keywords.txt`** — one keyword/phrase per line (case-insensitive,
  whole-token). Any page whose title or visible text matches is **dropped from
  the index**.
* **`blocklist_media.txt`** — one hex **SHA-256** per line. When the crawler
  downloads a media / non-text resource, it hashes the bytes and, on a match,
  **drops the page and flags the whole host `blocked`** (its indexed pages then
  disappear from search). This is the Ahmia-grade media-hash defense against
  known-abusive imagery (esp. CSAM). Tested end-to-end in `test_media_abuse.py`
  (a page referencing blocklisted media is not indexed and not searchable).

All three files ship **empty on purpose**. `#` comments and blank lines are
ignored.

The search server can also edit these lists at runtime for the AstrX admin:
**`POST /blocklist`** (`kind=host|keyword&value=…`, form-encoded) appends to the
right file and immediately reconciles the index. It is gated by a single bearer
token — set `--admin-token` (checked with `hmac.compare_digest`); unset ⇒ `403`.

> **You, the operator, MUST configure abuse filtering — in particular to exclude
> CSAM and other illegal content — before running any public index.** The crawl
> command prints a warning if both lists are empty. A page that hits either list
> is never stored (`test_crawl.py`, `test_abuse.py`), and blocked content is
> never returned by search (`test_search.py`).

---

## Darknet-only: optional I2P (`.i2p`) support

By default onioncrawler is **Tor-only** — `require_onion()` accepts nothing but
`.onion`. With `--enable-i2p` the anti-leak gate becomes **darknet-only**:
`is_darknet_host()` admits **only** `.onion` **or** `.i2p` (a 52-char
`*.b32.i2p` base32 destination, or any name ending in `.i2p`), and still refuses
clearnet / `localhost` / IP literals on every path.

Crucially, the onion anti-leak is **unchanged and the two networks never
cross-leak**. Each fetcher is locked to exactly one network:

* `TorSocksFetcher` gates every socket through `require_onion` (`.onion` only).
* `I2PHttpFetcher` (new) reaches eepsites through a local I2P **HTTP proxy**
  (default `127.0.0.1:4444`; absolute-form `GET` for http, `CONNECT`+TLS for
  https) and gates every socket through `require_i2p` (`.i2p` only).

So an onion crawl never opens an i2p or clearnet socket, and an i2p crawl never
opens an onion or clearnet socket — proven offline in `test_i2p.py` via the
`DirectFetcher(network=…)` transport (both directions, plus a full i2p crawl
that only ever contacts the eepsite host).

```
onioncrawler crawl --db i2p.db --enable-i2p --fetcher i2p \
    --i2p-proxy-host 127.0.0.1 --i2p-proxy-port 4444 \
    --seeds eepsites.txt --blocklist-hosts … --blocklist-keywords …
```

## Scheduled re-seed + curated seed list

A curated **known-onions** seed file is imported (validated darknet-only,
deduped, blocklist-checked) and its roots re-enqueued so the index keeps
rediscovering them (respecting the frontier caps + recrawl). A trapped/blocked
host is never re-seeded; a `dead` host is un-aged back to `active`.

```
onioncrawler reseed --db crawl.db --seed-list seeds.txt          # one-shot
onioncrawler crawl  --db crawl.db --seed-list seeds.txt --reseed-interval 21600
```

With `--reseed-interval N` (seconds) the crawler re-primes the curated list
every `N`s and keeps idle workers alive across an empty frontier. Tested in
`test_reseed.py` (re-enqueues done roots, adds new ones, respects caps).

## OpenSearch + backup

* **`/opensearch.xml`** — a well-formed OpenSearch descriptor served by the
  search server so a browser can add the engine (`test_admin_ops.py`).
* **`onioncrawler backup [--out FILE]`** — writes a consistent standalone DB
  copy via SQLite `VACUUM INTO` (a timestamped file by default).

---

## Extraction, index, and search

* **Extraction** (`extract.py`, stdlib `html.parser`): `<title>`, visible text
  (script/style/etc. dropped), `<a href>` links (resolved to absolute,
  `.onion`-only, canonicalized, enqueued through all trap rules),
  `rel=nofollow`, and robots meta.
* **Index**: SQLite **FTS5** (`search_index`), `rowid == pages.id`, columns
  `title, body` (+ unindexed `url, host`), `unicode61` tokenizer.
* **Ranking**: FTS5 `bm25(search_index, 10.0, 1.0)` — **title weighted 10× over
  body**. Snippets via FTS5 `snippet()` with `<mark>` highlighting.
* **Search server** (`search.py`, stdlib `http.server`, `ThreadingHTTPServer`):
  server-rendered **no-JavaScript** HTML form (`GET ?q=`), pagination, showing
  title + onion URL + snippet + last-seen; a JSON endpoint at
  `/api/search?q=`. Bound to `127.0.0.1`, no request logging, restrictive CSP
  (`default-src 'none'`), blocked hosts excluded at query time.

---

## Configuration

All knobs live in `config.py` (`Config` dataclass) and the important ones are
CLI flags. Notable defaults:

| Knob | Default | Meaning |
|------|---------|---------|
| `fetcher` | `tor` | `tor` or `direct` (test) |
| `tor_host` / `tor_port` | `127.0.0.1` / `9050` | Tor SOCKS proxy |
| `stream_isolation` | `True` | per-host Tor circuit |
| `allow_v2` | `False` | legacy 16-char onions (off) |
| `workers` | `4` | global concurrency cap |
| `crawl_delay` / `crawl_delay_jitter` | `3.0` / `1.5` | per-host politeness (s) |
| `lease_ttl` | `300` | seconds before a lease is reclaimed |
| `max_depth` | `8` | max link depth |
| `max_pages_per_host` | `500` | per-host budget |
| `max_total_pages` | `10000` | whole-run cap |
| `max_unique_urls` | `200000` | frontier backstop |
| `max_response_bytes` | `2 MB` | response size cap |
| `max_urls_per_template` | `50` | query-explosion cap |
| `pagination_numeric_cap` | `25` | numeric/date bomb cap |
| `max_urls_per_skeleton` | `200` | shape backstop cap |
| `max_path_segments` / `max_segment_repeats` | `12` / `3` | path traps |
| `dup_ratio_threshold` / `error_ratio_threshold` | `0.85` / `0.6` | auto-trap |
| `recrawl_ttl` | `7d` | refetch `done` only after this |
| `bind_host` / `bind_port` | `127.0.0.1` / `8802` | search server |

CLI overrides (crawl): `--fetcher --tor-host --tor-port --direct-map --allow-v2
--workers --crawl-delay --max-depth --max-pages-per-host --max-total-pages
--max-pages-this-run --no-robots --blocklist-hosts --blocklist-keywords -v`.

---

## Deploying with a real Tor daemon

Install Tor (`apt install tor`, `brew install tor`, etc.). A minimal `torrc`:

```
# /etc/tor/torrc  (or a private copy)
SocksPort 127.0.0.1:9050 IsolateSOCKSAuth KeepAliveIsolateSOCKSAuth
# Optional: a dedicated isolating port
# SocksPort 127.0.0.1:9052 IsolateSOCKSAuth
```

`IsolateSOCKSAuth` makes onioncrawler's per-host SOCKS username produce a
separate circuit per host. Then:

```bash
tor -f /path/to/torrc &                       # start Tor
python3 -m onioncrawler crawl --seeds seeds.txt --db crawl.db \
    --tor-host 127.0.0.1 --tor-port 9050 -v
```

Fill `seeds.txt` with real v3 `.onion` URLs (see `seeds.example`). **Populate
the abuse blocklists first.**

---

## Running the tests

```bash
cd /tmp/astrx-suite/onioncrawler
python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t . -v
```

The suite (no network, no Tor) proves:

* **`test_onion.py`** — validator accepts v3, rejects clearnet/invalid/wrong
  length/bad base32; v2 off by default, on by flag.
* **`test_socks.py`** — SOCKS5/1929 byte encoding matches the RFC layout
  (greeting, user/pass auth, CONNECT domain + big-endian port, always `ATYP=3`).
* **`test_canonical.py`** — canonicalization (host/port/fragment/tracking/dot
  segments/relative) and trap guards (too-deep, repeated-segment, cyclic path,
  pagination detection, template/skeleton keys).
* **`test_fetcher.py`** — non-onion hosts are refused with no socket opened
  (anti-leak), for both fetchers.
* **`test_abuse.py`** — host + keyword blocklists, file loading, whole-token
  matching, empty lists block nothing.
* **`test_crawl.py`** — a full crawl against the fixture **terminates** (does
  not hang in the trap), honors robots (`/secret` never fetched), decodes gzip +
  chunked, dedups duplicate pages, **drops the keyword-blocked page**, honors
  `noindex`, never contacts the blocked host or any clearnet host, and bounds
  every trap.
* **`test_resume.py`** — stop after K pages then restart: state persisted, no
  `done` URL refetched, same final page set as one run; plus lease-reclaim after
  a simulated crash.
* **`test_search.py`** — FTS5 search returns the expected page ranked, snippets
  are highlighted, title weighting works, pagination works, the HTML UI + JSON
  API return results, and the **blocklisted page is never returned**.

---

## Status & limitations

**Working (real, tested):** onion validator; hand-rolled SOCKS5 (no-auth +
user/pass, remote DNS); HTTP/1.1 with chunked/CL/gzip/deflate + redirect cap +
optional keep-alive connection reuse; onion-only anti-leak (two layers, redirects
re-validated before connect); crash-safe SQLite/WAL frontier with atomic leasing
and reclaim; resume; all nine trap-defense classes; robots + meta-robots +
X-Robots-Tag; abuse host/keyword filtering; FTS5 search with bm25 title
weighting, snippets, pagination, JSON API; full CLI.

**New in v1.1 (all tested):**

* **Discovery** — the v3/v2 `.onion` regex runs over page *body text* (not just
  `<a href>`), and `Sitemap:` directives in robots.txt are fetched + parsed
  (stdlib XML, entity-bomb-guarded) and enqueued.
* **Freshness + recrawl** — per-page `ETag`/`Last-Modified` are stored;
  conditional GETs (`If-None-Match`/`If-Modified-Since`) treat `304` as
  "unchanged, bump last-seen" (no re-index), and a per-page interval scheduler
  (`fetched_at + interval`, adaptive backoff on unchanged) re-leases due pages
  respecting per-host politeness — ending the write-once index.
* **Liveness + dead-onion aging** — per-host consecutive-failure + up/down
  history; hosts down across N recrawl cycles are demoted to `dead` (hidden from
  search, never leased, **never hard-deleted**) and revived on recovery / reseed.
* **Search filters/facets** — host, last-seen date range, language guess, a total
  count, and host/lang facets, in both the no-JS UI and the JSON API.
* **Link-graph authority** — inter-onion link edges are persisted and an offline
  PageRank-lite (`authority`) is blended into bm25 ranking.
* **Near-duplicate/mirror clustering** — 64-bit SimHash clusters mirrors and
  collapses fuzzy duplicates in results (the fuzzy complement to exact dedup).
* **Ops** — token-bucket rate limiting, Basic-auth admin actions
  (`/add` `/purge` `/recrawl`), `/metrics` + `/health`, structured JSON logs,
  and packaging (`pyproject.toml` console entry point, `Dockerfile`, systemd
  units, example `torrc`).

**Limitations / honest notes:**

* **No Tor in the sandbox.** The real SOCKS5→Tor transport (`TorSocksFetcher`)
  cannot be exercised end-to-end here; it is covered at the byte/encoding level
  and by anti-leak tests. Tests use `DirectFetcher` (localhost) by design.
* **HTTPS onions** are supported in `TorSocksFetcher` via stdlib `ssl` (cert
  verification off by default, since onion services self-authenticate;
  `verify_tls` to enable). This path is not exercised offline.
* **robots.txt** parsing covers the common `allow`/`disallow`/`crawl-delay`
  grammar with `*`/`$`; it is not a byte-perfect implementation of every
  non-standard extension.
* **Politeness** uses a host clock + jitter; robots `crawl-delay` is honored but
  capped to avoid delay-based tarpits.
* **Single SQLite database.** Workers share one connection under a lock; this is
  simple and crash-safe and fine at this scale (workers are I/O-bound), but it
  is not designed for multi-machine distributed crawling.
* **Abuse filtering is only as good as the operator's lists.** onioncrawler
  ships them empty and warns; keeping them current is your responsibility.
* This tool is for **legitimate research/indexing of public onion services**.
  Respect site operators, the law, and the abuse-filtering duty above.
